# Traefik no longer terminates TLS at all (see traefik.yaml.tftpl) -- Caddy
# handles TLS/ACME entirely now (Caddyfile.tftpl), so there's no `tls:`
# stanza needed here any more.
#
# RESOLVED 2026-08-22 (was "KNOWN ISSUE" here before): the investigation
# into "Traefik obtains a real cert but never serves it" (extensive --
# ruled out TLS store naming, tls.options, Traefik v3.1.7 vs v3.5.6,
# stale/duplicate port-443 listeners, acme.json permissions,
# defaultGeneratedCert) turned out to have two separate root causes, not
# one Traefik bug:
#   1. k3s ships its own default Traefik ingress controller, enabled by
#      default (`curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -` with no flags). Its
#      ServiceLB component binds host ports 80/443 directly via iptables
#      rules that take priority over Docker's own port publishing -- so
#      every test this whole investigation, including ones from the VM's
#      own loopback, was actually hitting that separate k3s-internal
#      Traefik pod (self-signed default cert) rather than the
#      docker-compose Traefik being debugged. Fixed by adding
#      `--disable=traefik` to the k3s install command in bootstrap.sh.tftpl.
#   2. Independent of (1): Traefik's own ACME-obtained certs, even once
#      genuinely reachable, still never made it from its cert store into
#      the actual TLS listener (confirmed via internal debug logs showing
#      successful "Adding certificate for domain(s)..." with no errors,
#      yet every live handshake still returned the internal self-signed
#      default cert) -- reproduced across per-host/wildcard,
#      freshly-issued/store-loaded, and ACME/static certs, on both
#      Traefik v3.1.7 and v3.5.6. This looks like a genuine Traefik defect;
#      not pursued further given (1) alone would have explained the
#      original symptom. Worked around by moving TLS termination to Caddy
#      entirely (see Caddyfile.tftpl) rather than filing/chasing an
#      upstream Traefik bug report.
